      "description": "Finding LCM of 12 and 18",
      "working": "LCM(12, 18) = 2² × 3² = 4 × 9 = 36\n∴ LCM of 12 and 18 is 36",
      "marks": 0.5
    },
    {
      "stepNumber": 3,
      "description": "Expressing 20 and 30 as product of prime factors",
      "working": "20 = 2 × 2 × 5 = 2² × 5¹\n30 = 2 × 3 × 5 = 2¹ × 3¹ × 5¹",
      "marks": 0.5
    },
    {
      "stepNumber": 4,
      "description": "Finding LCM of 20 and 30",
      "working": "LCM(20, 30) = 2² × 3¹ × 5¹ = 4 × 3 × 5 = 60\n∴ LCM of 20 and 30 is 60",
      "marks": 0.5
    }
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "Using the common division/ladder method instead of prime factorisation as specifically demanded by the question, resulting in loss of step marks.",
    "Confusing LCM with HCF by taking the smallest powers of common prime factors instead of the highest powers of all prime factors."
  ],
  "examTip": "Always express prime factors in exponential/index form (e.g., 2² × 3) and recall that LCM is the product of the greatest power of each prime factor involved in the numbers."
